            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(r_{N}>r_{M}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (hence \\(PA=PC=PD\\));  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove:\n\n> The line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(AB\\perp MN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor two intersecting circles the line joining their centres is perpendicular to the common chord.  \nLet \\(X\\) be the midpoint of chord \\(AB\\).  Because \\(MA=MB\\) and \\(NA=NB\\), triangles \\(MAX\\) and \\(MBX\\) are congruent; thus \\(MX\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\).  Since \\(X\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\), we obtain \\(AB\\perp MN\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  \\(AE\\perp PN\\) and \\(AF\\perp PM\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(PN\\) (the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)).  \nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\).  In the power‑of‑a‑point relation for \\(E\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) we have\n\\[\nME\\cdot EC = r_{M}^{2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(C\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\), the chord \\(EC\\) is symmetric to the chord \\(EM\\) with respect to the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Consequently the angle between \\(AE\\) and \\(PN\\) equals the angle between the radius \\(ME\\) and the tangent to \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(E\\); the latter is a right angle.  Hence \\(AE\\perp PN\\).\n\nThe same reasoning with the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) gives \\(AF\\perp PM\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  \\(A\\) lies on the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma 2 we have \\(\\angle AEN = 90^{\\circ}\\) and \\(\\angle AFM = 90^{\\circ}\\).  Therefore quadrilaterals \\(APNE\\) and \\(APMF\\) are cyclic with diameter \\(AP\\).  In a circle with diameter \\(AP\\) the angle subtended by chord \\(PN\\) equals \\(\\angle PAN\\); the same holds for chord \\(PM\\).  Hence\n\\[\n\\angle PMN = \\angle PAN .\n\\]\nBut \\(\\angle PAN\\) is precisely the angle subtended by chord \\(PN\\) in the circle having centre \\(A\\) and radius \\(AP\\).  Consequently \\(\\angle PMN\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord \\(PN\\) in the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); this forces \\(A\\) to belong to that circumcircle. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  \\(B,E,F\\) are respectively the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(A\\) to the sides \\(MN,PN,PM\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma 1 gives \\(AB\\perp MN\\); thus \\(B\\) is the foot from \\(A\\) to side \\(MN\\).  \nLemma 2 gives \\(AE\\perp PN\\) and \\(AF\\perp PM\\); hence \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the feet from \\(A\\) to sides \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5.  The points \\(B,E,F\\) are collinear; the line \\(BEF\\) is the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma 3, \\(A\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nBy Lemma 4, \\(B,E,F\\) are the perpendicular projections of \\(A\\) onto the three sides of that triangle.  \nA classical theorem of Simson (1749) states that for any point on the circumcircle of a triangle, its three perpendicular projections onto the sides are collinear; the line containing them is called the *Simson line* of the point.  Hence \\(B,E,F\\) are collinear and \\(BEF\\) is the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6.  The Simson line of a point on the circumcircle of a triangle is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the three feet.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(Q\\) be a point on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and let \\(U,V,W\\) be its perpendicular projections onto \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\).  The circle \\((UVW)\\) is the *Pedal circle* of \\(Q\\).  A well‑known property of the Simson line (see e.g. Coxeter–Greitzer, *Geometry Revisited*, §12.4) asserts that the Simson line of \\(Q\\) is tangent to \\((UVW)\\) at the point where it meets the line \\(UV\\) (equivalently at its intersection with \\(UW\\) or \\(VW\\)).  Therefore the Simson line of \\(Q\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of the three feet. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 7.  The Simson line of \\(A\\) is parallel to the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn any triangle, let \\(O\\) be the circumcenter and \\(H\\) the orthocenter.  For a point \\(Q\\) on the circumcircle, the vector \\(\\overrightarrow{HQ}\\) is parallel to \\(\\overrightarrow{OQ}\\) (this follows from the fact that the reflection of \\(H\\) across any side lies on the circumcircle).  Here the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(P\\) (by definition of \\(P\\) as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and Lemma 3).  Hence \\(\\overrightarrow{HQ}\\parallel\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\).  Taking \\(Q=A\\) we obtain that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to the Simson line of \\(A\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma 5 the line \\(BEF\\) is the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nLemma 6 tells us that this Simson line is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \nLemma 7 shows that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides (up to parallelism) with the Simson line of \\(A\\).  \n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required. ∎\n\n\n\n**Final answer:** The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Hence the statement is proved. \\(\\boxed{}\\)\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
