figuration becomes the classical one that appears in the Simson‑line theorem:\n\n   * the triangle whose sides are \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\omega\\) has vertices  \n     \\[\n     X=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\qquad\n     Y=\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\cap\\omega,\\qquad\n     Z=\\omega\\cap\\ell_{\\Omega};\n     \\]\n   * the point \\(A\\) is the **common point** of the three sides (it is the vertex of the triangle that is opposite the side \\(\\omega\\));\n   * the points \\(B',E',F'\\) are precisely the **feet of the perpendiculars** dropped from \\(A\\) to the three sides \\(MN\\;(=\\omega),\\;PN\\;(=\\ell_{\\Gamma})\\) and \\(PM\\;(=\\ell_{\\Omega})\\) of that triangle.\n\n   Hence \\(B',E',F'\\) are collinear – they lie on the **Simson line** of the point \\(A\\) with respect to the triangle \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).\n\n4. **Orthocenter after inversion.**  \n   The orthocenter of a triangle is sent by an inversion centred at a vertex to the **reflection of that vertex in the opposite side**.  \n   Therefore the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is mapped to the point \\(H'\\) which is the reflection of \\(A\\) in the side \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).  \n   Consequently the line \\(AH'\\) is **parallel** to the Simson line \\(B'E'F'\\).\n\n5. **Tangency in the inverted picture.**  \n   A classical property of the Simson line states that the Simson line of a point on the circum‑circle of a triangle is **tangent** to the circum‑circle of the three feet (the so‑called *pedal circle*).  \n   In our inverted picture the point \\(A\\) lies on the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) (the circum‑circle of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is mapped to that circle), therefore the Simson line \\(B'E'F'\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle \\((B'E'F')\\).\n\n6. **Returning to the original figure.**  \n   Inversion preserves tangency and parallelism (a line through the centre of inversion is invariant, and a line parallel to it stays parallel after 