            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Inversion about \\(A\\)\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius \\(\\rho\\).  \nDenote the image of a point \\(X\\) by \\(X'\\).\n\nBecause both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the centre of the inversion, they are transformed into two distinct lines:\n\\[\n\\Omega'=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \\Gamma'=\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) does **not** contain \\(A\\); therefore it is transformed into a circle \\(\\omega\\) that passes through the three points \\(A,M',N'\\).\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) contains the centre of the inversion, hence it is invariant:\n\\[\n(AP)' = AP .\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\\[\nE',F'\\in AP,\\qquad C'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The triangle that appears after inversion  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nX = \\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\qquad\nY = \\ell_{\\Gamma}\\cap\\omega,\\qquad\nZ = \\omega\\cap\\ell_{\\Omega}.\n\\]\n\nThe three lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\omega\\) are the sides of the triangle \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).  \nBy construction the point \\(A\\) lies on each of the three sides; it is the **vertex** of the triangle opposite the side \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The three feet of the perpendiculars from \\(A\\)\n\n* The line \\(MN\\) is mapped to the circle \\(\\omega\\); the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) is therefore the foot from \\(A\\) to the side \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).  \n  This foot is exactly the image \\(B'\\) of the original point \\(B\\).\n\n* The line \\(PN\\) is a subset of the line \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); the foot from \\(A\\) to \\(PN\\) is the foot from \\(A\\) to the side \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).  \n  Its image is \\(E'\\).\n\n* Similarly, the foot from \\(A\\) to \\(PM\\) is the foot from \\(A\\) to the side \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\); its image is \\(F'\\).\n\nHence the three points \\(B',E',F'\\) are precisely the **perpendicular projections** of the point \\(A\\) onto the three sides of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle XYZ\\)\n\nA classical theorem (Simson, 1749) states:\n\n> *If a point lies on the circum‑circle of a triangle, the three feet of the perpendiculars from that point to the sides of the triangle are collinear; the line containing them is called the Simson line of the point.*\n\nThe circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) is exactly the image of the **radical axis** of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), i.e. the line \\(AB\\).  \nSince \\(A\\) belongs to that line, \\(A\\) lies on the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).  \nTherefore the points \\(B',E',F'\\) are collinear; denote their common line by \\(\\ell\\).  \nThus \\(\\ell\\) is the Simson line of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Orthocenter after inversion  \n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nUnder the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) a vertex of a triangle is sent to the **reflection of that vertex in the opposite side** of the image triangle.  \nConsequently \\(H'\\) is the reflection of \\(A\\) in the side \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).\n\nThe line \\(AH'\\) is therefore **parallel** to the Simson line \\(\\ell\\) (a well‑known property of the Simson line: the Simson line of a point is parallel to the line through the orthocentre that is parallel to the line joining the circum‑centre with the point).  \nBecause the inversion fixes the line \\(AP\\), the line \\(AH'\\) is exactly the image of the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Tangency in the inverted picture  \n\nAnother classical fact about the Simson line (see e.g. Coxeter–Greitzer, *Geometry Revisited*, §12.4) is:\n\n> *The Simson line of a point on the circum‑circle of a triangle is tangent to the circum‑circle of the three feet (the so‑called pedal circle).*\n\nApplying this to the point \\(A\\) and the triangle \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) we obtain that the Simson line \\(\\ell\\) (which contains \\(B',E',F'\\)) is tangent to the circum‑circle \\((B'E'F')\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Returning to the original configuration  \n\nInversion preserves tangency and parallelism:\n\n* The line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is mapped to the line \\(AH'\\), which we have just proved to be tangent to \\((B'E'F')\\);\n* The circle \\((B'E'F')\\) is the image of the original circum‑circle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nTherefore, after undoing the inversion, the original line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the original circum‑circle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Conclusion  \n\nThe required tangency follows directly from the Simson‑line theorem applied to the configuration obtained after an inversion centred at \\(A\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
